## 9. Phase Five: Room Lifecycle, SSE, and Timer Cleanup

The current implementation uses in-memory rooms and SSE connections.

Inspect all room timers, bot timers, stream connections, and cleanup operations.

Implement safe lifecycle management.

Requirements:

- Remove disconnected SSE clients from room subscriber collections.
- Close streams cleanly when a client disconnects.
- Prevent memory leaks from abandoned rooms.
- Prevent stale bot timers from modifying deleted rooms.
- Clear room-specific timers when a room is deleted.
- Ensure timers do not keep abandoned games alive indefinitely.
- Avoid deleting active rooms merely because one player temporarily disconnects.
- Define a reasonable expiration policy for abandoned waiting rooms.
- Define a reasonable cleanup policy for completed games.
- Ensure cleanup cannot delete a newly created room that happens to reuse an identifier.
- Handle exceptions in asynchronous timer callbacks.
- Prevent duplicate timer scheduling.
- Do not broadcast private session credentials through SSE.

If automatic room cleanup is added, make the expiry configurable where useful and choose conservative defaults.

### SSE requirements

Verify:

- Appropriate response headers.
- Initial state delivery.
- State update delivery.
- Client disconnection handling.
- Reconnection behavior.
- Behavior when the room no longer exists.
- Behavior when the server restarts.

Avoid repeatedly sending unchanged state without a reason.

Do not add WebSockets unless SSE cannot reasonably support the existing requirements.

### Acceptance criteria

- Disconnected streams are cleaned up.
- Abandoned rooms eventually expire.
- Timers stop when rooms are deleted.
- Reconnecting players cannot impersonate other players.
- Server restarts do not leave phantom sessions or timers.
- No persistent memory growth occurs from ordinary room usage in basic stress testing.

